# Backend Engineering Constitution

## Purpose

This project is intended to be a long-lived enterprise application.

Every architectural decision MUST prioritize:

- Maintainability
- Readability
- Predictability
- Correctness
- Scalability
- Testability
- Explicitness

over

- Cleverness
- Premature optimization
- Code golfing
- Hidden abstractions
- Magic behavior

The objective is to produce software that remains understandable and maintainable after many years of development.

---

# General Principles

## Single Responsibility

Every class MUST have one reason to change.

Every function MUST perform one logical operation.

Every module MUST represent one domain or concern.

Large functions MUST be decomposed.

---

## Explicit Over Implicit

Prefer explicit code over hidden behavior.

Avoid "magic."

A developer unfamiliar with the project should understand what the code does without tracing multiple layers of indirection.

Avoid hidden side effects.

Never rely on undocumented framework behavior.

---

## Readability First

Readable code is preferred over short code.

Avoid dense one-liners.

Avoid deeply nested conditions.

Prefer descriptive variable names.

Prefer early returns.

Prefer composition over clever abstractions.

---

## Predictability

Every function should behave consistently.

The same input should always produce the same output unless explicitly documented otherwise.

Avoid hidden state.

Avoid global mutable state.

Avoid unexpected mutations.

---

## Enterprise First

Code should be written as though:

- another team will maintain it
- another service will consume it
- another developer will debug it
- another company will audit it

Never optimize for writing code quickly.

Optimize for maintaining code safely.

---

# SOLID

The entire project MUST follow SOLID principles.

Single Responsibility Principle

Open Closed Principle

Liskov Substitution Principle

Interface Segregation Principle

Dependency Inversion Principle

Violations should be considered architectural issues.

---

# DRY

Avoid duplicated business logic.

Small duplication is acceptable.

Large duplicated business logic is not.

Do not create abstractions too early.

The Rule of Three applies.

Only extract abstractions after the third duplication.

---

# KISS

Keep implementations simple.

Simple code scales.

Complexity should only exist when justified.

Avoid unnecessary abstraction layers.

Avoid unnecessary inheritance.

Avoid unnecessary metaprogramming.

---

# YAGNI

Do not build features that are not currently required.

Future extensibility is important.

Premature implementation is not.

The architecture should support extension without implementing speculative functionality.

This applies to speculative features and integrations with no current
consumer — not to the foundational structural conventions defined
elsewhere in this rule set (module layout, layering, the core exception
categories), which this project treats as fixed vocabulary rather than
built-ahead-of-need functionality.

---

# Composition

Prefer composition over inheritance.

Inheritance should be rare.

Inheritance should model true "is-a" relationships.

Utility inheritance is prohibited.

---

# Explicit Dependencies

Every dependency should be visible.

Hidden dependencies are prohibited.

Dependency injection should make dependencies obvious.

---

# Separation of Concerns

Every layer has one responsibility.

Routing

Business Logic

Persistence

Validation

Serialization

Authentication

Authorization

Configuration

Logging

Monitoring

should remain independent.

Business logic must never leak into infrastructure.

Infrastructure must never leak into business logic.

---

# Domain Driven Thinking

Business rules belong to the domain.

Frameworks are implementation details.

Database schemas are persistence details.

HTTP is a transport mechanism.

The domain should remain independent from all of them.

---

# Architecture

Router → Use Case → Repository → Database, composed through `app/api/`.
Full diagram and layer responsibilities: **`project.mdc`**.

No shortcuts are allowed. Every layer has a single responsibility.

---

# Business Logic

Business logic belongs ONLY inside Use Cases.

Business logic MUST NOT exist inside

Routers

Repositories

Models

Schemas

Validators

Utilities

Middleware

Configuration

---

# Database Independence

The business layer must not know SQLAlchemy exists.

The business layer must not know MySQL exists.

The business layer should work if another persistence layer replaces SQLAlchemy.

Repositories provide this abstraction.

---

# Framework Independence

FastAPI is an implementation detail.

The application should not depend on FastAPI concepts outside the presentation layer.

Domain logic should remain reusable.

---

# Predictable APIs

Every endpoint should behave consistently.

Naming

Responses

Errors

Validation

Pagination

Authentication

Authorization

must follow consistent conventions.

---

# Fail Fast

Detect invalid state as early as possible.

Never continue after detecting corrupted state.

Never silently ignore errors.

Never swallow exceptions.

---

# Defensive Programming

Assume

client input is invalid

network calls fail

database operations fail

files disappear

timeouts happen

dependencies become unavailable

Code should handle failure gracefully.

---

# Code Should Explain Itself

Good names are preferred over comments.

Comments explain WHY.

Code explains WHAT.

Never use comments to compensate for poor naming.

---

# Error Messages

Errors should be actionable.

Errors should explain

what failed

why it failed

how to fix it

Never expose internal implementation.

---

# Consistency

Consistency is more valuable than personal preference.

If two implementations solve the same problem, use the project standard.

Do not invent new patterns.

---

# Backward Compatibility

When a project integrates with an existing or legacy system, backward
compatibility becomes a priority.

Avoid breaking existing contracts.

Avoid unnecessary schema changes.

Prefer migrations over rewrites.

---

# Legacy Integration

When legacy database structures are in play, respect them.

Do not redesign legacy schemas without explicit approval.

Adapt new code to existing contracts whenever practical.

---

# Performance Philosophy

Do not optimize prematurely.

Measure first.

Optimize second.

Never sacrifice maintainability for insignificant performance gains.

Optimize only proven bottlenecks.

---

# Security Philosophy

Security is the default.

Never trust external input.

Validate everything.

Escape where appropriate.

Use least privilege.

Prefer secure defaults.

---

# Testing Philosophy

Code that cannot be tested is poorly designed.

Design for testability.

Dependencies should be injectable.

Business logic should be isolated.

Avoid hidden state.

---

# Documentation Philosophy

Architecture should be discoverable.

Complex decisions should be documented.

Public APIs should be documented.

Important trade-offs should be documented.

---

# AI Code Generation Rules

Generated code MUST follow the project architecture.

Generated code MUST NOT invent new architectural patterns.

Generated code MUST conform to existing conventions.

Generated code MUST prefer consistency over creativity.

When multiple valid implementations exist, choose the one already used within the project.

Never introduce unnecessary dependencies.

Never introduce unnecessary abstractions.

Never generate placeholder implementations unless explicitly requested.

Never generate TODO comments instead of implementations.

---

# Decision Priority

When multiple rules conflict, follow this priority:

1. Correctness
2. Security
3. Data Integrity
4. Maintainability
5. Readability
6. Consistency
7. Performance
8. Developer Convenience

---

# Non-Negotiable Rules

Never bypass the architecture.

Never duplicate business logic.

Never sacrifice readability for brevity.

Never optimize before measuring.

Never leak infrastructure into the domain.

Never expose persistence models outside repositories.

Never violate separation of concerns.

Always write code that another engineer can confidently modify years later.

This philosophy governs every file in this repository.